# 15. Atomic Operations ⭐⭐⭐

Atomics allow certain operations to occur without a traditional mutex.

In C:

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>

atomic_int counter;
```

Then:

```c
atomic_fetch_add(&counter, 1);
```

In C++:

```cpp
std::atomic<int> counter{0};

counter.fetch_add(1);
```

Atomics are useful for:

* Counters
* Flags
* Lock-free data structures
* Coordination between threads

---

# 16. Atomic ≠ Automatically Thread-Safe

This is important.

Making one variable atomic does **not** automatically make an entire data structure thread-safe.

For example:

```text
atomic<int> size
vector data
```

The size might be atomic while the vector itself is being modified unsafely.

Think about the **entire invariant**, not individual variables.

---

# 17. Compare-and-Swap (CAS) ⭐⭐

One of the fundamental operations behind lock-free algorithms.

Conceptually:

```text
if current_value == expected:
    current_value = new_value
```

Atomically.

Example:

```text
Expected = 10
New      = 20

Current = 10
    ↓
CAS succeeds
    ↓
Current = 20
```

But:

```text
Expected = 10
Current  = 15
    ↓
CAS fails
```

CAS allows threads to attempt updates without taking a traditional lock.

---

# 18. Lock-Free Programming

A lock-free data structure attempts to make progress without traditional mutex locking.

Example:

```text
Thread A ──────┐
               ↓
            Shared Data
               ↑
Thread B ──────┘
```

Instead of:

```text
LOCK
modify
UNLOCK
```

you might use:

```text
READ
 ↓
COMPUTE
 ↓
CAS
 ↓
success? ── yes → done
   │
   no
   ↓
retry
```

This is much harder to implement correctly.

Don't start here.

Master mutexes first.

---

# 19. Memory Ordering ⭐⭐⭐

This becomes important when learning atomics.

Learn these concepts:

* Relaxed
* Acquire
* Release
* Acquire-release
* Sequential consistency

The key idea:

> Atomicity and ordering are separate concepts.

An atomic operation can guarantee that a value is updated safely while providing different guarantees about how other memory operations are observed.

---

# 20. Acquire / Release

A common pattern:

```text
Thread A

write data
   ↓
release
   ↓
atomic flag
```

Thread B:

```text
acquire
   ↓
read data
```

The acquire/release relationship can establish that Thread B sees the writes that Thread A performed before the release.

You don't need to memorize the formal memory model immediately.

First understand:

```text
Thread A
   |
   | publish
   ↓
Atomic variable
   |
   | acquire
   ↓
Thread B
```

---

# 21. False Sharing ⭐⭐⭐

This is especially important for high-performance systems.

Suppose:

```text
Cache Line
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ counterA │ counterB          │
└──────────────────────────────┘
```

Thread A modifies `counterA`.

Thread B modifies `counterB`.

Even though the variables are logically independent, they may occupy the same CPU cache line.

The cache coherence system may repeatedly invalidate the cache line.

This causes unnecessary performance degradation.

### Solution

Separate frequently modified variables onto different cache lines.

In C++:

```cpp
struct alignas(64) Counter {
    std::atomic<int> value;
};
```

But don't blindly use `alignas(64)` everywhere.

Understand **why** you're doing it.

---

# 22. Cache Coherence

Modern CPUs have multiple caches.

```text
Core 1 → L1/L2
Core 2 → L1/L2
Core 3 → L1/L2
Core 4 → L1/L2
           ↓
          RAM
```

If multiple cores modify shared data, the CPU needs to maintain a coherent view of memory.

Learn:

* Cache lines
* Cache invalidation
* Cache coherence
* Locality
* False sharing

This becomes extremely important for performance-sensitive systems.

---

# 23. Thread Pools

Instead of constantly creating new threads:

```text
Task → create thread
Task → create thread
Task → create thread
```

use a fixed pool:

```text
             Thread Pool
        ┌──────┬──────┬──────┐
Queue → │ T1   │ T2   │ T3   │
        └──────┴──────┴──────┘
```

Workers repeatedly:

```text
get task
   ↓
execute task
   ↓
get next task
```

Build one yourself.

